 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "The whole chapter on one page",
   "text": "Everything in this unit is one story: **molecules** make a **gas**; the gas can receive **heat** and do **work**; the **first law** keeps the energy books; **processes** and **engines** put it to use; the **second law** tells us which way things go.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Molecules and gas laws",
   "text": "Billions of molecules hitting the walls give the **pressure**; their average kinetic energy is the **temperature**. Together they give **Boyle, Charles, Gay-Lussac** and the ideal gas equation \\( PV = nRT \\).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 1
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "foc": 2
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 2000
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Work and internal energy",
   "text": "A gas that expands pushes the piston and does **work**: the area under the P–V graph. The energy stored in the molecular motion is the **internal energy** \\( U \\), which rises with temperature.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 3
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "foc": 4
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 2000
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "The first law joins them",
   "text": "Heat in goes into raising U and doing work: \\( Q = \\Delta U + W \\). This is **conservation of energy** for the gas, and every process in the unit is an application of it.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 5
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Four special processes",
   "text": "Hold one thing fixed and the first law simplifies: constant volume (**W = 0**), constant pressure, constant temperature (**ΔU = 0**), or no heat flow (**Q = 0**, adiabatic).",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 6
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s5",
   "title": "Engines",
   "text": "Chain the processes into a **cycle** and you have an **engine**: heat in, work out, heat rejected. The **Carnot cycle** (two isotherms, two adiabats) gives the best efficiency \\( 1 - T_c/T_h \\). Run backwards, it is a refrigerator.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 7
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s6",
   "title": "The second law and entropy",
   "text": "Energy conservation is not enough. The **second law** says no engine turns all its heat into work and heat never flows cold to hot by itself. **Entropy**, the measure of disorder, never decreases in a spontaneous change.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 8
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s7",
   "title": "The links",
   "text": "Each arrow is an idea passed down: the kinetic picture explains the gas laws and U; both feed the first law; the first law gives the four processes; the processes build engines; the second law limits them.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "foc": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "formulas": [
  {
   "title": "The chapter in four equations",
   "tex": "PV = nRT \\quad Q = \\Delta U + W \\quad \\eta = 1 - \\dfrac{T_c}{T_h} \\quad \\Delta S = \\dfrac{Q}{T}",
   "text": "Gas law, first law, Carnot efficiency, entropy change."
  }
 ],
 "challenges": [
  {
   "id": "c1",
   "type": "choice",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "Which idea links **heat, internal energy and work**?",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "a",
     "label": "The first law"
    },
    {
     "id": "b",
     "label": "Boyle's law"
    },
    {
     "id": "c",
     "label": "The Carnot cycle"
    },
    {
     "id": "d",
     "label": "Entropy"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "a",
   "hint": "It is a statement of energy conservation.",
   "explain": "The first law: Q = ΔU + W.",
   "set": {
    "foc": 5
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c2",
   "type": "choice",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "Which idea tells us that **no engine can be 100% efficient**?",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "a",
     "label": "The first law"
    },
    {
     "id": "b",
     "label": "The second law"
    },
    {
     "id": "c",
     "label": "Charles's law"
    },
    {
     "id": "d",
     "label": "The ideal gas equation"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "b",
   "hint": "Energy is conserved even for a perfect engine.",
   "explain": "The second law (Kelvin–Planck) forbids converting all heat into work.",
   "set": {
    "foc": 8
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "type": "choice",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "In which process is the **work zero** because the volume cannot change?",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "a",
     "label": "Isochoric"
    },
    {
     "id": "b",
     "label": "Isobaric"
    },
    {
     "id": "c",
     "label": "Isothermal"
    },
    {
     "id": "d",
     "label": "Adiabatic"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "a",
   "hint": "W = PΔV.",
   "explain": "Constant volume means ΔV = 0, so W = 0.",
   "set": {
    "foc": 6
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c4",
   "type": "choice",
   "title": "Now you try",
   "prompt": "Which quantity is largest in an ideal reversible engine working between two given temperatures?",
   "options": [
    {
     "id": "a",
     "label": "Efficiency (Carnot value)"
    },
    {
     "id": "b",
     "label": "Heat rejected"
    },
    {
     "id": "c",
     "label": "Total entropy production"
    },
    {
     "id": "d",
     "label": "Work lost to friction"
    }
   ],
   "answer": "a",
   "hint": "No engine can beat it.",
   "explain": "The Carnot engine reaches the maximum possible efficiency; its total entropy production is zero.",
   "set": {
    "foc": 7
   }
  }
 ],
 "takeaways": [
  "Molecules → **gas laws** and **internal energy** → **first law** → **processes** → **engines** → **second law and entropy**.",
  "The first law (**Q = ΔU + W**) is the energy book-keeping; the second law decides the direction.",
  "Every animation in this unit is one box on this map."
 ],
 "notes": [
  {
   "title": "How to use this map",
   "text": "Open any other animation in the unit, then come back here to see where it sits. Each *Connects to* note points to the next box."
  }
 ]
}